  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does a vertical-lift bridge work?",
      "a": "The whole deck span is raised straight up between two towers while staying level. Wire ropes over pulleys at the tower tops connect it to counterweights, so it rises like a lift.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a lift bridge?",
        "How does a lifting bridge go up?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What do the counterweights do?",
      "a": "They hang on the other end of the ropes and weigh about as much as the span. They balance it, so as the span rises the counterweights fall, and little energy is needed.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does a lift bridge have counterweights?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a sheave?",
      "a": "A sheave is a large grooved pulley wheel. On a lift bridge the ropes pass over sheaves at the top of each tower.",
      "asks": [
        "What are the wheels on top of the towers?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What powers a vertical-lift bridge?",
      "a": "Electric motors, on the span or in the towers, wind the ropes or turn the sheaves. Because the load is balanced, the motors can be surprisingly small.",
      "asks": [
        "How is a lift bridge raised?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "When is a vertical-lift bridge better than a bascule?",
      "a": "Lift bridges suit long, heavy spans such as railway bridges, because the deck stays level and is held at both ends. Their limit is that ships must fit under the raised span.",
      "asks": [
        "Lift bridge versus bascule bridge"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The Arthur Kill Vertical Lift Bridge between New Jersey and Staten Island, New York, has a lift span of about 170 metres, among the longest in the world.",
    "A lift bridge's counterweights roughly equal the weight of its span, so the motors mainly overcome friction and wind."
  ],
